(;W[fm]C[pwsiegel\: which patterns do you mean specifically? stuff like the kosumi in the upper left
pwsiegel\: ?
Trevoke\: I mean.. let's see.. The counter-pincer in the SE corner...
Trevoke\: The tenuki from P4, though Q11 is obviously a modern variant
Trevoke\: The P17 attachment / direction shift after the Q14 probe, though usually this happened later in the game
]
(;B[gk]
(;W[gm]C[Trevoke\: The D15 kosumi is also a 17th century movee, though, that is quite true \:D 
Trevoke\: The H17 pincer after D15 is also an old pattern, though with k16 present obviously it's a completely different ball game
pwsiegel\: interesting, i don't know my go history too well - but yeah it's very cool that people are bringing back old ideas
Trevoke\: E15-E14-F15-D17-D18 is, I'm relatively certain, an old pattern as well, though I'd have to check old games to see how it worked out 
pwsiegel\: i always liked the d15 kosumi, but everyone used to say it was bad before AI
Trevoke\: P4 and the subsequent O4 are also quite old
Trevoke\: Sorry, I meant Q3 and the subsequent O4
pwsiegel\: "AI analysis of old go games" would be a great youtube channel
pwsiegel\: (go games / go moves)
Trevoke\: You're better off checking Michael Redmond's reviews of Dosaku and Shusaku games, that's the best you're gonna find out there at the moment
Trevoke\: He does use AI to enhance his reviews
]
(;B[ik]
(;W[pj]
(;B[oo]
(;W[pp]C[Trevoke\: Yeah, the kosumi is a great move; the evolution of our understanding moved away from it as we focused more on "speed" and for ... About 70 years, I think (?) we thought it was too slow, particularly compared to what we got with the 4-4, but as often goes when theoretical understanding evolves, it doesn't evolve in every direction at once \:D
]
(;B[oj]
(;W[pk]
(;B[om]
(;W[ok]
(;B[mo]C[pwsiegel\: I think AI likes a different kind of speed\: make a shape that can't be severely attacked as quickly as possible, and then go for a big point elsewhere
]
(;W[nn]
(;B[nm]
(;W[mn]C[pwsiegel\: the kosumi is not very ambitious, but it is very hard to attack
]
(;B[mm]
(;W[ln]
(;B[nk]C[Trevoke\: The kosumi is ... The height of flexible moves. Both stones can go in two directions.
Trevoke\: It's a fantastically flexible pattern that buys you a TON of time in the fuseki
]
(;W[ql]C[MXHero\: to complicate the AI discussion\: the fact that robots like both the D15 kosumi and the D14 knight's move at different times hints at something deeper than the speed or flexibility of either. Not that I know what that knowledge is, but it's an interesting question
Trevoke\: This double hane with O9 is a pattern that is reminiscent of Dosaku's light approach to center control - see https\://youtu.be/STr1o1JrC20?t=1181
pwsiegel\: hmmm i can feel myself getting sucked into a rabbit hole
]
(;B[rn]C[Trevoke\: @MXHero - I have spent too much time looking at old games and not enough time asking the AI what it wants to do; do you have samples (or can you guide me towards finding my own) of situations when the AI wants D14 instead?
MXHero\: [object Object\]
]
(;W[rm]
(;B[qn]C[MXHero\: @Trevoke I'm unfortunately just speaking very generally; I have heard high dan players mentioning how much AI likes the knight's move sometimes. 
]
(;W[qi]
(;B[ph]C[Trevoke\: I'll have to look at it
Trevoke\: I feel like I should gather a bunch of high dans and explain to them what information they're looking at when they look at AI suggestions
MXHero\: But you can compare it to how AI has almost completely deprecated the old "normal" 3-3 invasion of 4-4 joseki
]
(;W[qh]
(;B[qg]
(;W[rg]C[MXHero\: AI decided, in that invasion situation, that a variety of different josekis are almost always better than the old way; but in the kosumi vs knight's move question, they both seem like possible moves (contextually)
pwsiegel\: I still don't understand why the old 3-3 invasion joseki is bad, and I play it all the time
pwsiegel\: This is a thing I should probably study that
Trevoke\: The AI has found that "the way it evaluates continuations through the game, based on the policy network's restrictions on the Monte Carlo Tree Search" translates to "it will not really play this pattern"
Trevoke\: In practice, as humans, we have to fall back on things like "what sente moves exist on these shapes later on, and how do they change the board position", as well as "do we end in sente or not", etc.
]
(;B[rl]
(;W[rk]
(;B[sm]
(;W[rj]
(;B[rf]
(;W[rh]C[Trevoke\: The key point in all this, though, is that the AI's decisions are *restricted* by its policy network, so we don't necessarily know what it thinks are the best moves
Trevoke\: In another one of his reviews, Michael Redmond indicated that he sometimes had to let the AI study the board position for quite a while before it would realize that Dosaku's move was the best one
]
(;B[im]
(;W[io]
(;B[je]
(;W[kg]C[Trevoke\: All this to say that when we just blindly follow "what the AI says" we're actually fitting ourselves into a narrow groove, and if we don't understand that then we are taking away crucial creativity from ourselves, and trading it for a very very specific approach to reasoning, which assesses the quality of a move based on all the moves that actually follow it -- which isn't really how us humans are able to play this game.
Trevoke\: Okay, stepping down from the sandbox
